A||||9||Pike||Insulating Exhaust Tubing or Muffin Fans?||||1||
Z||000000||Pike||10-07-2001||09:23 PM||||Have any of you tried the racing insulation tape on your exhaust tubing to try to cool down the engine compartment? As I understand it, you wrap the exhaust tubing with this stuff that comes in a roll. It is an insulation tape that they use on race cars to cool areas near the driver's feet etc. I'd like to figure out a way to drop the temp a few degrees. I'm always worried that the heat is going to melt a cable harness or other components. What about muffin fans? Has anyone tried those?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||10-07-2001||09:33 PM||||Don't know about you, but I prefer my muffins warm.<P>Havent had a problem with wiring harnesses melting.  Of course, there is always a chance that they will given the correct conditions, but the black corrguated tubing they are in is supposed to be sufficient for proection.<P>The best thing you can do is inspect all the wiring looms and make sure some of the plastic hasnt melted.  Replace all that has.  I bought some heat reflective tape from summit especially for wiring looms and such, and used it on the large wiring harness that comes through the firewall and is right near the exhaust manifold.  This harness has reflective tape on it from the factory, but mine was wearing away so I replaced it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pike||10-07-2001||09:52 PM||||Yep, the big bundle at the firewall is my biggest worry. Mine is wrapped pretty good and the reflective tape is still pretty good. I just think there must be a way to improve upon this condition. The engine will run better, last longer and I think overall it would make the vehicle a bit more comfortable to drive. I seen some that were so bad that they actually discolored the paint on the deck lid. Wouldn't it be nice if it were average temps back there?<BR>Anybody got any other ideas?<BR>Thanks, <BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000003||BlackDragon||10-07-2001||11:15 PM||||exhaust wrap is a great thing. it does a great job of reducing heat. but it is difficult to use. you have to pull the exhaust manifold in order to wrap it, and the reast of the exhaust will not be any easier. i am in the middle of a total rebuild, and my entire exhaust system is lying on the floor of my shop in tact. after making a few modifications i plan on wraping my exhaust from the manifold to the resonator. should give about a 30-40 percent drop in compartment temperature. <BR>what i dont understand is why pontiac designed the exhaust system to be like a cradle for the engine. that wont make your engine run hot... no of course not....DUH!!!<P>------------------<BR>We are all different for a reason.....<BR>you can read a persons soul through there eyes.....<BR>he who argues with a fool is probably one himself.....<BR>big cars may be fast, but i can corner.....<P>BlackDragonsEmail@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leper||10-08-2001||12:46 AM||||It's not like they had alot of choice. I guess that they could have ran a set of zoomies out of the decklid or something.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||falconhulk||10-08-2001||08:19 AM||||hmmmmmm I use it on my mustang but it has headers!  I would think it would be kind of hard to wrap a manifold!.  I guess wrapping the exhaust pipe might help but the thing is that wrap holds moisture and causes rust.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Smoooooth GT||10-08-2001||08:38 AM||||I have my manifold's and Ypipe wrapped in some stuff called Thermo-tec. It work's really well...  some people on  here have said before that it hurt's(makes hotter), but that makes no sense to me though...  it does make it ALOT cooler iunder the decklid...   just my 2cent's...  Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L V/6<BR>UNDER CONSTRUCTION   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Joe Torma||10-08-2001||09:54 AM||||Had the wrap on my car...seemed to make things a bit cooler.  It provides a nice steam effect around the lid when it got wet!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-08-2001||08:40 PM||||There is some info about this in my cave....<P>I'm not a big fan of wrap. Works ok on headers but it's a troll to get a manifold wrapped and get it to look nice. Some of the header makers aparantly don't like it much. I seem to remember that some header makers void you warantee if you wrap them.<P>One issue with a wrap is that if it's not done well, it can cause very uneven heating of the wrapped part and the part will run hotter than normal at the same time. This can lead to accelerated cracking of the part because differant areas are heating and coolaing at vastly differant rates.<P>The result is similar to crash cooling a manifold by dumping water on it. It that case it shrinks unevenly... end result is the part breaks. This is really common on V6 fron manifold and 4 cyl manifold because water on the deck lid floods onto it when the lid is opened.<P>There is a DIY applicable thermal coating from TechLine Coatings. It's probably not as effective as Jet-Hot's or TechLine's profesionally applied products but it's a hell of allot less expensive and easier to get uniform results. A big plus of coatings is they don't take up space in the already cramped engine bay.<P>In any case you'll need to tear down some or all the exhaust to apply whatever you choose.<P>As for muffin fans.... They generally aren't made to tolerate outdoor use. They probably won't last very long. When they croak they will block the grates and further restrict air flow. You need something made to take car use. There's notes about posible choices for that option in the cave as well.<P>As mentioned above... make sure the wires are properly wrapped and routed. That will do allot.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||10-09-2001||04:13 AM||||Get them JetHot coated, *great* stuff.  Not just good, GREAT!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
